Step 4: Variant Calling (bcftools mpileup + call)

### Commands


The BAM alignment files and reference genome from the ../04/bwamem2/ directory were soft-liked to the 05_bcftools directory. 
```
ln -s ../04_bwamem2/Sample_A_R.sorted.bam .
ln -s ../04_bwamem2/Sample_B_R.sorted.bam .
ln -s ../04_bwamem2/Sample_C_R.sorted.bam .
ln -s ../04_bwamem2/Sample_D_R.sorted.bam .
ln -s ../04_bwamem2/sacCer3.fa .
```

BAM files being so massive need to be index to aid with the alignment data. This would allow for tools such as bcftools to locate and jump
```
samtools index Sample_A_R.sorted.bam
samtools index Sample_B_R.sorted.bam
samtools index Sample_C_R.sorted.bam
samtools index Sample_D_R.sorted.bam
```

In this stage 'bcftools mileup' was used on each sample to compare the aligned reads to the reference genome position-by-position to summarize  how the bases match up and output the results into the .bcf file format. Below is an example with Sample_A_R.
```
bcftools mpileup \
  -f sacCer3.fa \
  -a AD,ADF,ADR,DP,SP \
  -q 5 \
  -d 250 \
  -Ou \
  -o Sample_A_R.pileup.bcf \
  Sample_A_R.sorted.bam
```

The 'bcftools stats' command generates a text-based summary for the individual sample's raw .bcf and saves the output as a text file. 
This would be conducted for each sample. 
```
bcftools stats Sample_A_R.raw.bcf > Sample_A_R.raw.stats.txt
```

Alternatively, to process multiple sample BAM files simultaneously using a list text file to compile alignment data against the reference genome. 
```
bcftools mpileup \
  -Ou \
  -q 5 \
  -b bam_list.txt \
  -f sacCer3.fa \
  -a AD,ADF,ADR,DP,SP | \
bcftools call \
  -m \
  -v \
  -Oz \
  -o yeast_samples_raw.vcf.gz
```

This command runs 'bcftools stats' on the raw multi-sample VCF file to compute variant counts and depth distribution then saves the results into a text report. 
```
bcftools stats yeast_samples_raw.vcf.gz > yeast_samples_raw.stats.txt
```

